//   "text": "NYPA RFQ Q26-7741TK (MAY 29) SETS A HIGH BAR FOR THE AP1000 LSR PATHWAY.\n\nA credible developer submission has to clear five gates. Most firms will fail at least three.\n\nThe OEM Anchor\n• AP1000 is the only large-scale reactor with a completed U.S. FOAK build. Vogtle 3 & 4.\n\n• Westinghouse is the sole AP1000 OEM. There is no credible LSR submission without them.\n\n• Cameco and Brookfield own Westinghouse. The capital relationship runs deep.\n\nThe EPC Credential Gate\n• NYPA screens for prior nuclear construction experience across multiple delivery phases.\n\n• Bechtel is the only U.S. EPC with completed AP1000 construction on its resume (Vogtle).\n\n• Any serious AP1000 bid is built around that reference point.\n\nThe Licensing Screen\n• Prior NRC engagement is effectively a binary qualification criterion.\n\n• A developer without documented licensing history needs a partner who has it.\n\n• This alone eliminates most post-2020 nuclear entrants.\n\nThe Finance Structure\n• IRA Investment Tax Credit eligibility requires construction start before 2033.\n\n• NYPA wants risk and upside shared between developer and owner, not a pure fixed-fee contract.\n\n• The equity stack and compensation model are part of qualification, not just the later RFP.\n\nThe Integrator's Actual Job\n• NYPA is not looking for a reactor vendor or a pure EPC firm.\n\n• The selected developer must coordinate OEM, EPC, licensing, offtake, financing, and community engagement under a shared-risk structure.\n\n• That full-ecosystem capability is what this RFQ is really screening for.\n\nVery few players can credibly clear all five.\n\n$CCJ $BAM $URNM $LEU",
