//   "text": "AI reduces the cost of producing work, which increases the amount of work companies attempt. The labor market impact depends on whether the demand expansion is larger than the labor-saving effect. In software, sales, marketing, security, analytics, and operations, AI may create a project explosion before it creates a headcount collapse.\n\nThat is the key upgrade.\n\nThe best frame is:\n\nAI does not simply replace workers. It changes the bottleneck.\n\nBefore AI, the bottleneck was often producing the first draft, the first design, the first script, the first model, the first campaign, the first codebase, or the first analysis.\n\nAfter AI, the bottleneck becomes deciding what should exist, validating what was built, securing it, integrating it, maintaining it, selling it, measuring it, and being accountable for it.\n\nThat is where your argument becomes very strong.\n\nFactual pressure test\n\nThe current data does not support a clean “AI has already destroyed white-collar jobs” story. Reuters reported this week that Bridgewater sees widespread AI job-displacement risk as limited in the near term, partly because compute capacity and economic resilience are slowing labor-market disruption. Bridgewater also said fewer than 20% of U.S. firms reported using AI in any business function over a two-week period, and more than 90% of AI-using firms reported no employment effect over the prior six months; among firms where AI did affect staffing, more reported headcount increases than decreases.\n\nSoftware is also not showing up in official projections as a dying occupation. BLS projects overall employment of software developers, QA analysts, and testers to grow 15% from 2024 to 2034, with about 129,200 openings per year, and specifically cites continued expansion of AI, IoT, robotics, automation, cybersecurity, and software-containing products as drivers of demand.  BLS also projects computer and information technology occupations overall to grow much faster than average, with about 317,700 openings per year.\n\nNear-term tech hiring data also supports the “demand is returning / evolving” version. CompTIA’s April 2026 analysis said new tech job postings hit a three-year high, with 271,483 new tech-occupation postings and more than 575,000 active tech postings; active software developer and engineer postings were up 32.3% since January, systems engineers and architects were up 42.7%, and cybersecurity roles were up 23.2%.  CompTIA’s 2026 workforce report also said AI-skill hiring reached nearly 275,000 active job postings in January 2026.\n\nBut you should include the counterweight: the data is not uniformly bullish for every worker. Anthropic’s labor-market research found no systematic increase in unemployment for highly AI-exposed workers, but it did find tentative evidence that hiring into exposed occupations has slowed for workers aged 22–25, including an estimated 14% drop in job-finding rates versus 2022.  Anthropic also found computer programmers, customer-service representatives, and financial analysts among the most exposed roles.\n\nSo the cleanest factual line is:\n\nThe evidence so far points less to mass unemployment and more to job reshaping, slower junior hiring in exposed roles, and rising demand for people who can own AI-accelerated systems.\n\nThat is the defensible version.\n\nStronger rewritten version\n\nThe jobs data continues to suggest something more complicated than the “AI kills all jobs” https://t.co/HbuRxYsYda is not just a labor substitute. It is also a demand https://t.co/fSGq2ADIwU is the clearest example. Everyone assumed coding would be the first profession crushed by AI. But what seems to be happening is different: AI makes it cheaper to build software, so companies try to build far more of it. More internal tools. More dashboards. More automations. More agents. More customer-facing features. More integrations. More experiments. More data pipelines. More security work. More compliance systems. More legacy migrations. More prototypes that suddenly become production problems.Non-technical people can now build rough software faster than ever. But that does not eliminate engineers. It creates a giant pile of things that eventually need engineering ownership.Someone has to understand the system.\nSomeone has to secure it.\nSomeone has to maintain it.\nSomeone has to debug it when the AI-generated code breaks at 2 a.m.\nSomeone has to upgrade the dependencies.\nSomeone has to connect it to the company’s actual data.\nSomeone has to make it reliable enough for customers.\nSomeone has to decide whether the thing should exist in the first https://t.co/BFso1zqZSb lowers the cost of creating code, but it increases the surface area of software inside the economy.That is jobs.The same pattern can show up in sales. Agents can research more accounts, write more outreach, qualify more leads, and create more personalized follow-up. That does not necessarily mean fewer salespeople. It may mean companies can profitably cover more market segments, more geographies, more verticals, and more customers than before. The human salesperson becomes more valuable where trust, negotiation, judgment, and relationship depth https://t.co/RWVxjadhr6 has the same dynamic. AI makes campaigns cheaper to launch, which means companies launch more campaigns, test more variations, enter more niches, personalize more messages, and need more people to manage brand, taste, compliance, measurement, attribution, and channel strategy.The mistake is assuming AI only reduces the labor required for today’s workload.The bigger question is what happens when AI makes the workload itself https://t.co/js0P8a2wGz many functions, the future is not “same company, same output, fewer workers.”It may be: same company, dramatically more output, different workers, different skills, and much more software-mediated https://t.co/fTARyvlclX will destroy some tasks. It may compress some junior roles. But it may also create far more work around integration, supervision, security, operations, customer expansion, and system ownership.The labor-market question is not whether AI can do pieces of the job.The question is whether AI expands the ambition of companies faster than it reduces the headcount needed per unit of output.\n\nThe missing master frame: Jevons paradox for work\n\nThe most important concept to add is Jevons paradox.\n\nWhen a technology makes a resource cheaper to use, total usage can rise instead of fall. More efficient coal engines increased coal consumption because they made coal-powered work more economical across more use cases.\n\nAI may do the same thing for software, content, research, analysis, and sales activity.\nThe line:\nAI makes work cheaper, so companies demand more work.\nOr sharper:\nThe cost per software project falls, so the number of software projects explodes.\nThat is the heart of your argument.\nThe best version:\nAI is Jevons paradox for white-collar work. The cheaper it becomes to create code, campaigns, analysis, and outreach, the more companies attempt to create.\n\nThis makes the argument much more sophisticated than “AI creates jobs.”\n\nThe engineering point, sharpened\n\nYour engineering example is the strongest part. Make it more precise.\n\nAI changes engineering from:\n\nwriting every line of code\n\nto:\n\nowning systems that now generate, mutate, integrate, and maintain much more code than before.\n\nThat means the engineer’s job moves up the stack.\n\nThe future software engineer is less:\n\ntypist of code\n\nand more:\n\narchitect, reviewer, debugger, security owner, integration lead, reliability engineer, product translator, codebase steward, agent supervisor, and technical accountable party.\n\nBest line:\n\nAI does not eliminate software engineering. It eliminates the idea that software engineering is mostly typing code.\n\nAnother:\n\nWhen code becomes cheap, engineering judgment becomes expensive.\n\nAnother:\n\nThe bottleneck moves from code generation to system ownership.\n\nThat last line is probably the most important.\n\nThe “shadow software” problem\n\nThis is a missing element that would make your argument much stronger.\n\nAI will let non-technical employees build apps, automations, dashboards, agents, scripts, forms, and internal tools. This is powerful, but it creates a new enterprise problem: shadow software.\n\nShadow software means business teams create tools that become important before engineering knows they exist.\n\nThat creates jobs because someone has to deal with:\n\nsecurity vulnerabilities, bad permissions, broken automations, duplicate systems, data leakage, compliance failures, dependency drift, unmaintained scripts, hallucinated logic, undocumented workflows, and “temporary” prototypes becoming business-critical infrastructure.\n\nBest line:\n\nAI lets everyone create software, which means companies will need more people who can make software safe.\n\nAnother:\n\nThe first AI app is free. The maintenance contract is not.\n\nAnother:\n\nVibe coding creates vibe debt. Engineers get hired to pay it down.\n\nThis is a very strong addition.\n\nThe real software job boom: maintenance, security, and ownership\n\nPeople obsess over AI writing code. That is the least interesting part.\n\nThe durable work is what comes after code exists.\n\nMore AI-generated software means more:\n\ncode review\n security audits\n dependency upgrades\n observability\n testing\n documentation\n data modeling\n permissions\n incident response\n SRE work\n migration work\n integration work\n compliance review\n technical product management\n architecture governance\n cost optimization\n agent monitoring\n\nBLS already frames software developers and QA analysts as involved in the full process of creating software, including requirements, security, design, testing, and identifying risks; that description supports the idea that the occupation is broader than raw code writing.\n\nLine to add:\n\nAI can write code. It cannot be on call, hold the pager, answer the auditor, own the roadmap, or explain the outage to the customer.\n\nAnother:\n\nThe more software AI creates, the more humans are needed to decide which software deserves to live.\n\nThe counterargument you should include\n\nDo not ignore junior engineers. That is where the bearish case has teeth.\n\nThe strongest counterargument is:\n\nAI may increase total technical work while reducing the need for traditional entry-level coding labor.\n\nThat means the labor market can be bullish for experienced engineers and brutal for juniors at the same time.\n\nAnthropic’s data points exactly there: no clear unemployment spike in exposed occupations, but tentative evidence of reduced hiring for workers aged 22–25 entering exposed jobs.\n\nAdd this:\n\nThe risk is not that software engineering disappears. The risk is that the apprenticeship layer gets squeezed.\n\nThat is a major missing element.\n\nThe better version:\n\nAI may not kill the senior engineer. It may kill the old path to becoming a senior engineer.\n\nThis makes your argument more honest and stronger.\n\nThe hidden labor-market mechanism\n\nThe AI labor market probably will not move like this:\n\nAI appears → companies fire everyone.\n\nIt will move more like this:\n\nAI appears → teams ship more → managers stop backfilling some roles → junior hiring slows → experienced generalists become more valuable → software surface area explodes → security/reliability/maintenance burden rises → companies hire different roles than before.\nSo the line is:\nAI job loss may show up first as jobs that never get posted, while AI job creation shows up as roles that did not exist five years ago.\nThat is far more nuanced.\nThe sales example, upgraded\nYour sales point is good but needs one extra layer: AI creates more leads, but also more noise.\nIf every company uses agents to research prospects, write emails, and personalize outreach, inboxes get flooded with plausible machine-written messages. That makes trust more valuable, not less.\nSales jobs shift from:\nfinding basic prospect data and writing generic emails\nto:\naccount strategy, relationship-building, qualification, negotiation, channel design, customer education, procurement navigation, solution engineering, and trust creation.\nBest line:\nAI can generate infinite outreach. It cannot generate infinite trust.\nAnother:\nWhen everyone can personalize at scale, the scarce skill becomes knowing who is actually worth talking to.\nAnother:\nSales teams may hire more because AI expands the reachable market, but the humans move closer to qualification, trust, and closing.\n\nThe marketing example, upgraded\n\nAI makes content cheap. That does not automatically kill marketing.\n\nIt can create more marketing work because companies will run:\n\nmore campaigns, more micro-segments, more variants, more landing pages, more customer journeys, more A/B tests, more influencer briefs, more localization, more videos, more SEO experiments, more lifecycle campaigns, more sales enablement, more brand assets.\n\nBut when content becomes infinite, taste becomes scarce.\n\nMarketing jobs shift from:\n\nproducing basic copy and images\n\nto:\n\npositioning, strategy, brand trust, taste, campaign architecture, audience research, compliance, measurement, distribution, and creative direction.\n\nBest line:\n\nAI makes content infinite. That makes attention, taste, and distribution more valuable.\n\nAnother:\n\nThe marketer’s job moves from making assets to deciding which assets deserve to exist.\n\nAnother:\n\nThe AI content explosion creates a new scarcity: believable differentiation.\n\nApply the thesis across functions\n\nEngineering\n\nAI creates more software projects, more integrations, more security issues, more maintenance, more architecture debt, and more system ownership needs.\n\nLine:\n\nWhen everyone can build, engineers become the building inspectors.\n\nSales\n\nAI lets teams process more leads, research more accounts, personalize more outreach, and cover more niches.\n\nLine:\n\nThe sales bottleneck moves from prospecting to trust.\n\nMarketing\n\nAI makes campaigns cheaper, so companies launch and test far more campaigns.\n\nLine:\n\nThe marketing bottleneck moves from asset creation to taste and measurement.\n\nCustomer support\n\nAI handles tier-one tickets, but may increase expectations for instant service and create more escalations around complex issues.\n\nLine:\n\nAutomation raises the service baseline, which raises customer expectations.\n\nLegal and compliance\n\nAI drafts faster, but regulated work requires review, audit, policy, version control, and accountability.\n\nLine:\n\nAI can draft the contract. It cannot absorb the liability.\n\nFinance\n\nAI can produce more analysis, forecasts, models, and reconciliations, but someone must validate assumptions and own the numbers.\n\nLine:\n\nAI makes more models. Humans still own the bad assumptions.\n\nHR\n\nAI can screen, draft, summarize, and train, but hiring, conflict, performance, culture, and legal fairness require human accountability.\n\nLine:\n\nThe more AI enters HR, the more governance HR needs.\n\nSecurity\n\nAI creates more code, more agents, more identities, more APIs, more integrations, and more attack surface.\n\nLine:\n\nAI is not just a productivity tool. It is an attack-surface multiplier.\n\nThe real thesis: AI creates “work inflation”\n\nThis is an obscure but powerful phrase.\n\nAI may not reduce work. It may inflate the amount of work companies believe is possible.\n\nBefore AI:\n\n“We cannot build that; it would take too long.”\n\nAfter AI:\n“Why have we not built that yet?”\n\nThat changes management expectations.\n\nThe new company posture becomes:\n\nlaunch more experiments\n cover more customers\n build more internal tools\n analyze more data\n personalize more experiences\n automate more workflows\n refresh more content\n monitor more systems\n test more ideas\n\nBest line:\n\nAI does not just automate the backlog. It expands the backlog.\n\nAnother:\n\nThe most underrated effect of AI is ambition inflation.\n\nAnother:\n\nOnce the cost of trying falls, companies try everything.\n\nThe “opposite effect” should be reframed\n\nYour ending says:\n\n“AI is going to have the opposite effect that lots of people thought on jobs.”\n\nI would make it more precise:\n\nAI may have the opposite effect in high-elasticity functions where lower production cost unlocks much more demand.\n\nThat is the exact mechanism.\n\nAI is more likely to increase jobs where:\n\nlatent demand is huge\n quality matters\n integration is complex\n liability remains human\n the work creates new revenue\n the output needs maintenance\n customers want more, not just cheaper\n the company has many unrealized projects\n\nAI is more likely to reduce jobs where:\n\ndemand is fixed\n work is repetitive\n outputs are standardized\n quality can be automatically checked\n customers do not need human trust\n there is little maintenance burden\n the company can remove headcount without losing revenue\n\nThat is the framework you need.\n\nBest line:\n\nAI creates jobs where demand is elastic and destroys jobs where demand is fixed.\n\nThis is one of the most important additions.\n\nThe labor-market equation\n\nAdd this:\n\nNet job effect = demand expansion − labor intensity reduction − coordination cost + maintenance burden + regulatory burden + trust requirement.\n\nThat is the whole model.\n\nSimpler version:\n\nAI creates work when it expands the market faster than it reduces labor per unit.\n\nThat sentence should be in the post.\n\nThe “engineers only” line needs nuance\n\nYour draft says:\n\n“Effectively only engineers are going to be the ones doing that work.”\n\nThat is too narrow.\n\nBetter:\n\nEngineers will not be the only people building. They will be the people responsible when the building matters.\n\nThat is much better.\n\nNon-technical people will build prototypes, automations, landing pages, scripts, workflows, internal apps, dashboards, and agents. Engineers will be needed when those things touch customers, payments, regulated data, security, production systems, or company-critical workflows.\n\nBest line:\n\nEveryone will create software. Engineers will own software.\n\nAnother:\nAI democratizes building, but it does not democratize accountability.\nThe missing “accountability premium”\nThis is a genius-level idea.\nAs AI makes production cheaper, accountability becomes more valuable.\nWho signs off?\nWho approves the release?\nWho owns the customer outcome?\nWho is liable if the agent sends the wrong email?\nWho secures the workflow?\nWho explains the failure?\nWho decides whether the AI-generated output is true?\nLine:\nAI cheapens production and raises the premium on accountability.\nAnother:\n\nThe future job market rewards people who can own outcomes, not just produce outputs.\n\nThis applies across engineering, sales, marketing, finance, law, support, HR, and operations.\n\nThe “quality bar rises” effect\n\nAI changes what “good enough” means.\n\nIf every engineer can ship faster, every team expects faster iteration.\nIf every marketer can generate 100 variants, one asset is no longer enough.\nIf every salesperson can research 200 accounts, shallow knowledge is no longer acceptable.\nIf every analyst can build a model, the value shifts to better assumptions and better judgment.\n\nLine:\n\nAI does not just make workers more productive. It raises the minimum acceptable output.\n\nAnother:\n\nYesterday’s high output becomes tomorrow’s baseline.\n\nThis supports job creation because companies may hire more to meet the new standard of output.\n\nThe “AI creates managerial burden” point\n\nAI does not remove management. It often creates more things to supervise.\n\nAgents create tasks.\nTasks create review queues.\nReview queues create bottlenecks.\nBottlenecks create new roles.\nNew roles create governance.\n\nLine:\nThe bottleneck moves from doing work to supervising work.\nAnother:\nAI does not eliminate management. It miniaturizes management onto every worker’s desk.\nIn software, one engineer may supervise multiple agents. In marketing, one strategist supervises many campaign generators. In sales, one rep supervises many lead-research and outreach agents.\nThe “more projects than ever” point, sharpened\nYour best line is:\n“Most companies now have far more software projects than ever before because of AI.”\n\nMake it more vivid:\n\nEvery department now has a software wishlist that used to be impossible. Finance wants reconciliation agents. HR wants onboarding agents. Sales wants account-research agents. Legal wants contract-review agents. Support wants triage agents. Marketing wants campaign factories. Operations wants dashboard automation. Product wants experiment engines. Executives want internal copilots.\nThen:\nThat is not less engineering work. That is a thousand small systems asking to become real.\nExcellent line:\nAI turns every department into a product team.\nThis may be the best sentence in the entire argument.\nThe hidden category: AI operations jobs\n\nAI creates a new class of work that did not exist at scale before:\n\nAI workflow architect\nDesigns how agents fit into business processes.\n\nAgent operations manager\nMonitors agent performance, failures, cost, and escalations.\n\nEvaluation engineer\nBuilds tests to determine whether AI output is good enough.\n\nModel router / inference optimizer\nChooses which model handles which task at which cost.\n\nAI security engineer\nDefends against prompt injection, data leakage, agent abuse, and tool misuse.\n\nAI compliance lead\nManages regulatory, audit, retention, and fairness requirements.\n\nHuman-in-the-loop supervisor\nApproves high-risk actions and handles exceptions.\n\nSynthetic QA analyst\nTests generated content, code, support responses, and workflows.\n\nAI cost / TokenOps analyst\nTracks cost per completed workflow and prevents runaway inference spend.\n\nAI product owner\nOwns internal AI tools as actual products, not experiments.\n\nBest line:\n\nThe AI labor boom will not look like old job titles growing linearly. It will look like every function acquiring an AI operations layer.\n\nThe critical caveat: not all jobs benefit\n\nThe argument should not pretend everyone wins.\n\nAI may reduce demand for:\n\nbasic content mills, low-level data entry, rote QA, simple summarization work, basic first-draft copywriting, generic customer-service scripts, simple spreadsheet cleanup, low-complexity coding tickets, transcription, templated design, commodity research, and junior work whose main value was producing first drafts.\n\nSo include:\n\nAI will hurt people whose job is mostly producing low-accountability first drafts. It will help people whose job is turning messy situations into reliable outcomes.\n\nThat is the balanced version.\n\nStronger short version for X\n\nThe jobs data continues to point to a more complicated AI story than “software engineers disappear.”Software may actually be the best example of the opposite https://t.co/4PNfPAH8L4 makes code cheaper to produce, which means companies try to build far more software. More internal tools, more dashboards, more automations, more customer features, more agents, more integrations, more experiments, more migrations, more security work.Non-technical people can get surprisingly far with AI now. But eventually someone has to understand what got built, connect it to real systems, secure it, maintain it, debug it, upgrade it, monitor it, and own it when it breaks.That is engineering https://t.co/fTARyvlclX lowers the cost of creating software, but it increases the surface area of software inside the economy.The same pattern shows up in sales and marketing. If agents let companies process more leads, research more accounts, launch more campaigns, test more segments, and personalize more outreach, companies may not simply hire fewer people. They may expand the amount of work they attempt.The mistake is assuming AI only reduces labor per task.The bigger effect may be that AI expands the number of https://t.co/J1U4ZXa13N creates jobs where demand is elastic and destroys jobs where demand is fixed.The future is not “no engineers, no salespeople, no marketers.”It is more software, more campaigns, more automation, more systems, more supervision, more maintenance, more security, and more accountability.The bottleneck moves from producing outputs to owning outcomes.\n\nMore aggressive version",
